1. what is Facade Design Pattern?
   Facade Design Pattern is a Structural Design Pattern that provides a simplified interface to a complex subsystem.

2. what is builder design pattern?
   The Builder Design Pattern is a Creational Design Pattern used to create complex objects step by step.
   It is especially useful when a class has multiple instance variables, including mandatory and optional fields.
   Instead of creating multiple constructors with different parameter combinations, Builder Pattern allows us to set
   only the required fields and optionally configure the remaining fields.

3. what is Factory Design Pattern?
   The Factory Design Pattern is a Creational Design Pattern used to encapsulate object creation logic.
   Instead of allowing the client to create objects directly using the new keyword, we provide a Factory class that
   decides which object needs to be created based on the given input.

4. what is strategic design pattern?
   The Strategy Design Pattern is a Behavioral Design Pattern that defines a family of algorithms, encapsulates each
   algorithm into a separate implementation, and allows us to select one algorithm at runtime.

   In simple words, we have one common interface and multiple implementations, and based on the requirement, we choose
   which implementation to execute.

5. what is Observer Design pattern?
   The Observer Design Pattern is a Behavioral Design Pattern that establishes a one-to-many relationship between
   objects.
   When one object (Subject) changes its state, it automatically notifies all the registered dependent objects
   (Observers)
   In simple words, when one object undergoes a change, it notifies multiple interested objects.

6. what is Decorator Design Pattern?
   The Decorator Design Pattern is a Structural Design Pattern used to add additional functionality or responsibilities
   to an existing object dynamically without modifying its original class.

7. what is Adapter Design pattern?
   The Adapter Design Pattern is a Structural Design Pattern that allows two incompatible interfaces to work together.


